  "items": [
   {
    "id": "def-cg-parabolic-quotient-and-two-sided-minima",
    "kind": "definition",
    "title": "Standard parabolic subgroups, descent-free one- and two-sided representatives, parabolic and reflection subgroups",
    "deps": [
     "def-hh-coxeter-matrix-word-group-and-length",
     "def-cg-canonical-reflection-homomorphism",
     "thm-hh-parabolic-minimal-representatives-and-length-additivity",
     "def-generated-subgroup",
     "def-coset",
     "def-group",
     "thm-hh-coxeter-exchange-deletion-and-faithfulness"
    ],
    "statement": "Let $(S,m)$ be a finite Coxeter matrix, let $W$ be the presented group with length function $\\ell$ ([[def-hh-coxeter-matrix-word-group-and-length]]), and let $T=\\{wsw^{-1}:w\\in W,\\ s\\in S\\}$ be the set of reflections of $W$ ([[def-cg-canonical-reflection-homomorphism]]). Throughout, $I$ and $J$ denote subsets of $S$.\n\n**(1) Standard parabolic subgroups.** For $I\\subseteq S$ put\n$$W_I:=\\langle s:s\\in I\\rangle\\le W,$$\nthe subgroup generated by $I$ ([[def-generated-subgroup]]); $W_I$ is the **standard parabolic subgroup** of $(W,S)$ of type $I$. Thus $W_\\emptyset=\\{1\\}$ and $W_S=W$. By [[thm-hh-parabolic-minimal-representatives-and-length-additivity]] (1),(2): for every $w\\in W$ one has $w\\in W_I$ if and only if $S(w)\\subseteq I$, where $S(w)$ is the (well-defined) set of letters of any reduced expression of $w$; $(W_I,I)$ is a Coxeter system; its intrinsic length function agrees with $\\ell|_{W_I}$; and $W_I\\cap S=I$.\n\n**(2) Descents and the one-sided quotients.** For $w\\in W$ put\n$$D_L(w):=\\{s\\in S:\\ell(sw)<\\ell(w)\\},\\qquad D_R(w):=\\{s\\in S:\\ell(ws)<\\ell(w)\\};$$\neach of $\\ell(sw)-\\ell(w)$ and $\\ell(ws)-\\ell(w)$ lies in $\\{\\pm1\\}$ ([[thm-hh-coxeter-exchange-deletion-and-faithfulness]] (1)). Define\n$$W^I:=\\{w\\in W:\\ell(ws)>\\ell(w)\\text{ for all }s\\in I\\}=\\{w\\in W:D_R(w)\\cap I=\\emptyset\\},$$\n$${}^IW:=\\{w\\in W:\\ell(sw)>\\ell(w)\\text{ for all }s\\in I\\}=\\{w\\in W:D_L(w)\\cap I=\\emptyset\\}.$$\nConventions are fixed here: in ${}^IW$ the subset $I$ is tested on the left, in $W^I$ it is tested on the right; $\\ell(sw)$ means left multiplication by $s$ and $\\ell(ws)$ right multiplication. The interpretation of these sets as sets of coset representatives is not part of the definition; it is quoted from [[thm-hh-parabolic-minimal-representatives-and-length-additivity]] (3) and re-examined in [[thm-cg-parabolic-intersections-and-coset-factorization]] (3): every $w\\in W$ has a unique factorization\n$$w=u\\,d\\qquad(u\\in W_I,\\ d\\in{}^IW),\\qquad \\ell(w)=\\ell(u)+\\ell(d),$$\nwhere $d$ is the unique element of minimal length in the left coset $W_Iw$, and it satisfies $\\ell(ud)=\\ell(u)+\\ell(d)$ for all $u\\in W_I$; symmetrically every $w\\in W$ has a unique factorization\n$$w=d\\,v\\qquad(d\\in W^I,\\ v\\in W_I),\\qquad \\ell(w)=\\ell(d)+\\ell(v),$$\nwhere $d$ is the unique element of minimal length in the right coset $wW_I$, and it satisfies $\\ell(dv)=\\ell(d)+\\ell(v)$ for all $v\\in W_I$. Thus ${}^IW$ consists exactly of the elements of minimal length in the left cosets $W_Iw=\\{uw:u\\in W_I\\}$ and $W^I$ consists exactly of the elements of minimal length in the sets $wW_I=\\{wv:v\\in W_I\\}$. Inversion gives $W^I=({}^IW)^{-1}$, because $\\ell(x)=\\ell(x^{-1})$ and $(sw)^{-1}=w^{-1}s$.\n\n**(3) The two-sided quotients.** Define\n$${}^IW^J:={}^IW\\cap W^J=\\{w\\in W:\\ell(sw)>\\ell(w)\\text{ for all }s\\in I\\ \\text{ and }\\ \\ell(ws)>\\ell(w)\\text{ for all }s\\in J\\};$$\nthus ${}^\\emptyset W^J=W^J$, ${}^IW^\\emptyset={}^IW$ and ${}^\\emptyset W^\\emptyset=W$, and the members of ${}^IW^J$ are exactly the elements of $W$ with no left descent in $I$ and no right descent in $J$. Whether ${}^IW^J$ is a set of representatives of the double cosets $W_IwW_J$ is again a theorem, not a definition; it is proved in [[thm-cg-double-coset-unique-minimum-and-normal-form]].\n\n**(4) Parabolic subgroups and reflection subgroups.** A subgroup $H\\le W$ is a **parabolic subgroup** of $(W,S)$ if\n$$H=wW_Iw^{-1}\\qquad\\text{for some }w\\in W,\\ I\\subseteq S;$$\na parabolic subgroup with $w=1$ is *standard*. A subgroup $H\\le W$ is a **reflection subgroup** if $H=\\langle H\\cap T\\rangle$, i.e. if $H$ is generated by the reflections it contains. Every parabolic subgroup is a reflection subgroup, since $wW_Iw^{-1}=\\langle wsw^{-1}:s\\in I\\rangle$ and $wsw^{-1}\\in T$ for all $s\\in S$, $w\\in W$. No converse is asserted: it is not claimed here that a subgroup generated by reflections is parabolic, and the companion examples page computes test cases for the two distinctions created by this definition — standard parabolic versus parabolic, and reflection subgroup versus parabolic subgroup.",
    "strategy": "**Well-definedness.** $W_I$ is the subgroup generated by a subset of a group and exists by [[def-generated-subgroup]]; $W_\\emptyset=\\{1\\}$ is the trivial subgroup and $W_S=W$. The sets $W^I$, ${}^IW$ and ${}^IW^J$ are subsets of $W$ specified by a predicate and are therefore well-defined sets; no selection principle is used anywhere in this definition. The two displayed factorizations of (2) and the description of $W^I$, ${}^IW$ as sets of minimal representatives are quoted from [[thm-hh-parabolic-minimal-representatives-and-length-additivity]] (1)-(3), which proves them from the deletion and exchange properties before they are used here; the inversion identity $W^I=({}^IW)^{-1}$ uses $\\ell(x)=\\ell(x^{-1})$, $(sw)^{-1}=w^{-1}s$ ([[def-group]]) and the equivalence $\\ell(sw)<\\ell(w)\\Leftrightarrow\\ell(w^{-1}s)<\\ell(w^{-1})$. The only genuinely two-sided assertion is deferred: this item introduces ${}^IW^J$ as a set of descent-free elements and asserts nothing about double cosets; the property that every double coset $W_IwW_J$ contains exactly one element of ${}^IW^J$, and that this element is its minimum, is established in [[thm-cg-double-coset-unique-minimum-and-normal-form]], the later proof that justifies the definition for its applications (recorded in `justified_by`). The inclusion 'parabolics are reflection subgroups' in (4) is immediate from the definitions, since a conjugate $wsw^{-1}$ of a simple reflection is a reflection. No use of the Axiom of Choice is made; all constructions are subsets and subgroups of the fixed group $W$.",
    "sources": {
