   {
    "id": "thm-cg-parabolic-intersections-and-coset-factorization",
    "kind": "theorem",
    "title": "Intersections of standard parabolics, the parabolic root subsystem, and global minimality of coset representatives",
    "deps": [
     "def-cg-parabolic-quotient-and-two-sided-minima",
     "def-hh-coxeter-matrix-word-group-and-length",
     "thm-hh-coxeter-exchange-deletion-and-faithfulness",
     "thm-hh-parabolic-minimal-representatives-and-length-additivity",
     "def-cg-real-coxeter-form-and-reflection",
     "def-cg-canonical-reflection-homomorphism",
     "lem-cg-reflection-representation-descends-and-root-norms",
     "thm-cg-root-sign-and-simple-reflection-positivity",
     "thm-cg-root-length-criterion-and-faithfulness",
     "thm-cg-root-inversion-formulas-and-strong-exchange",
     "def-linear-combination-and-span",
     "def-generated-subgroup",
     "def-group"
    ],
    "statement": "Let $(S,m)$, $W$, $\\ell$ and $I,J\\subseteq S$ be as in [[def-cg-parabolic-quotient-and-two-sided-minima]], so that $W_I$, $W^I$, ${}^IW$ and the descents $D_L,D_R$ have the meaning fixed there. Let $V=\\mathbb R^S$ with its Coxeter form $B$ and positive cone $V_+=\\{\\sum_{s\\in S}\\lambda_se_s:\\lambda_s\\ge0\\}$ ([[def-cg-real-coxeter-form-and-reflection]]), let $\\rho:W\\to\\mathrm{GL}(V)$ be the canonical reflection homomorphism with root system $\\Phi=\\Phi_+\\sqcup\\Phi_-$ ([[def-cg-canonical-reflection-homomorphism]], [[thm-cg-root-sign-and-simple-reflection-positivity]] (2)), and for $I\\subseteq S$ put\n$$V_I:=\\operatorname{span}\\{e_s:s\\in I\\},\\qquad \\Phi_I:=\\{\\rho(w)e_s:w\\in W_I,\\ s\\in I\\}$$\n([[def-linear-combination-and-span]]); here $V_\\emptyset=\\{0\\}$ and $\\Phi_\\emptyset=\\emptyset$.\n\n**(1) Intersections.** For all $I,J\\subseteq S$,\n$$W_I\\cap W_J=W_{I\\cap J}.$$\n\n**(2) Parabolic root subsystems.** For every $I\\subseteq S$ one has $\\rho(w)V_I=V_I$ for all $w\\in W_I$; consequently\n$$\\Phi_I=\\Phi\\cap V_I=\\{\\alpha\\in\\Phi:\\alpha\\text{ is a real linear combination of the simple roots }e_s,\\ s\\in I\\}.$$\nMoreover $\\Phi_I=\\Phi_I^+\\sqcup\\Phi_I^-$ with $\\Phi_I^\\pm:=\\Phi_I\\cap\\Phi^\\pm=\\Phi^\\pm\\cap V_I$, and the reflections lying in $W_I$ are exactly the elements $t_\\alpha\\in T$ with $\\alpha\\in\\Phi_I^+$ in the root-reflection dictionary of [[thm-cg-root-inversion-formulas-and-strong-exchange]] (1).\n\n**(3) Coset minima are global minima.** Let $I\\subseteq S$, $w\\in W$, and let $d\\in W^I$ be the unique element of $W^I\\cap wW_I$, i.e. the unique $d\\in W^I$ with $w\\in dW_I$ ([[def-cg-parabolic-quotient-and-two-sided-minima]] (2)). Then\n$$\\ell(d)\\le\\ell(x)\\quad\\text{for every }x\\in wW_I,\\qquad\\text{and}\\qquad \\ell(d)=\\ell(x)\\iff x=d;$$\nequivalently\n$$W^I=\\{d\\in W:\\ell(d)\\le\\ell(dv)\\text{ for all }v\\in W_I\\},\\qquad {}^IW=\\{d\\in W:\\ell(d)\\le\\ell(vd)\\text{ for all }v\\in W_I\\}.$$\nThus each set $wW_I$ contains exactly one element of $W^I$, namely its unique element of minimum length, and symmetrically each left coset $W_Iw$ contains exactly one element of ${}^IW$, namely its unique element of minimum length.",
    "strategy": "**(1)** Here $\\ell$ is the word length of the presented group ([[def-hh-coxeter-matrix-word-group-and-length]]). By [[thm-hh-parabolic-minimal-representatives-and-length-additivity]] (1), $W_I=\\{w\\in W:S(w)\\subseteq I\\}$ and similarly for $J$; hence $w\\in W_I\\cap W_J$ if and only if $S(w)\\subseteq I$ and $S(w)\\subseteq J$, if and only if $S(w)\\subseteq I\\cap J$, if and only if $w\\in W_{I\\cap J}$. The converse inclusion is immediate from the definition of generated subgroups ([[def-generated-subgroup]]).\n**(2)** For $s\\in I$ the reflection formula of [[def-cg-real-coxeter-form-and-reflection]] (3) gives $\\rho(s)e_s=-e_s$ and $\\rho(s)e_t=e_t+c_{st}e_s$ for $t\\neq s$; in particular $\\rho(s)V_I=V_I$, and since the $s\\in I$ generate $W_I$ ([[def-generated-subgroup]]), $\\rho(w)V_I=V_I$ for all $w\\in W_I$. Hence $\\rho(w)e_s\\in\\Phi\\cap V_I$ for $w\\in W_I$, $s\\in I$, i.e. $\\Phi_I\\subseteq\\Phi\\cap V_I$.\nFor the converse, let $\\varphi\\in\\Phi\\cap V_I$. Replacing $\\varphi$ by $-\\varphi$ if necessary we may assume $\\varphi\\in\\Phi_+$ (both $\\Phi$ and $V_I$ are stable under negation); by the sign criterion [[thm-cg-root-sign-and-simple-reflection-positivity]] (2) all coefficients of $\\varphi$ in the basis $(e_s)_{s\\in S}$ are $\\ge0$, and $\\varphi\\in V_I$ says that its support lies in $I$:\n$$\\varphi=\\sum_{r\\in I}\\lambda_re_r,\\qquad \\lambda_r>0\\ \\ (r\\in\\operatorname{supp}\\varphi).$$\nLet $t:=t_\\varphi\\in T$ be the reflection with root $\\varphi$, where $T$ is the reflection set of [[def-cg-canonical-reflection-homomorphism]]; it exists and is unique by the dictionary [[thm-cg-root-inversion-formulas-and-strong-exchange]] (1)(iv). We prove $t\\in W_I$ by induction on $\\ell(t)$. If $|\\operatorname{supp}\\varphi|=1$ then $\\varphi=\\lambda_re_r$ for some $r\\in I$; since every root has $B$-norm one ([[lem-cg-reflection-representation-descends-and-root-norms]] (3)), $1=B(\\varphi,\\varphi)=\\lambda_r^2$, so $\\varphi=e_r\\in\\Phi_I$ and $t=s_r\\in W_I$. Assume $|\\operatorname{supp}\\varphi|\\ge2$. Using bilinearity of $B$ and $B(\\varphi,\\varphi)=1$,\n$$1=B(\\varphi,\\varphi)=\\sum_{r\\in I}\\lambda_r\\,B(\\varphi,e_r),$$\nso some $r\\in I$ has $B(\\varphi,e_r)>0$. Put $\\varphi':=t(\\alpha_{s_r})=\\alpha_{s_r}-2B(\\varphi,\\alpha_{s_r})\\varphi$, a root in $\\Phi\\cap V_I$, because $t$ acts by the reflection formula of [[def-cg-real-coxeter-form-and-reflection]] (3) and preserves both $\\Phi$ and $V_I$. Its coefficient at each $r'\\in\\operatorname{supp}\\varphi\\setminus\\{r\\}$ equals $-2B(\\varphi,e_r)\\lambda_{r'}<0$, and that set is nonempty as $|\\operatorname{supp}\\varphi|\\ge2$; by the sign criterion (2) of [[thm-cg-root-sign-and-simple-reflection-positivity]], $\\varphi'\\in\\Phi_-$. Put $\\psi:=\\rho(s_r)\\varphi=\\varphi-2B(\\varphi,e_r)e_r\\in\\Phi\\cap V_I$; its coefficients at the nonempty set $\\operatorname{supp}\\varphi\\setminus\\{r\\}$ are the $\\lambda_{r'}>0$, so $\\psi\\in\\Phi_+$ by the sign criterion (2) of [[thm-cg-root-sign-and-simple-reflection-positivity]]. By the conjugation formula of the dictionary (1)(ii), the reflection with root $\\psi$ is\n$$t_\\psi=s_r\\,t\\,s_r.$$\nSince $t(\\alpha_{s_r})=\\varphi'\\in\\Phi_-$, the root-length criterion [[thm-cg-root-length-criterion-and-faithfulness]] (1) gives $\\ell(ts_r)=\\ell(t)-1$; since $\\ell$ is invariant under inversion, also $\\ell(s_rt)=\\ell(t)-1$. Moreover $\\rho(s_rt)e_{s_r}=\\rho(s_r)\\varphi'$ has, at each $r'\\in\\operatorname{supp}\\varphi\\setminus\\{r\\}$, the negative coefficient $-2B(\\varphi,e_r)\\lambda_{r'}$ of $\\varphi'$ (the reflection $\\rho(s_r)$ alters only the coefficient of $e_r$), so it is a root with a strictly negative coefficient, hence lies in $\\Phi_-$ by the sign criterion; the root-length criterion applied to $w:=s_rt$ therefore gives $\\ell(t_\\psi)=\\ell(s_rts_r)=\\ell(s_rt)-1=\\ell(t)-2$. The induction hypothesis applies to $\\psi\\in\\Phi_+\\cap V_I$ and yields $t_\\psi\\in W_I$; then $t=s_rt_\\psi s_r\\in W_I$ because $s_r\\in I$. So every element of $\\Phi\\cap V_I$ is the root of a reflection of $W_I$, and the asserted equality $\\Phi_I=\\Phi\\cap V_I$ follows. The decomposition $\\Phi_I=\\Phi_I^+\\sqcup\\Phi_I^-$ only restates the definitions of $\\Phi_I^\\pm$ together with (2) of [[thm-cg-root-sign-and-simple-reflection-positivity]]. Finally, if $\\alpha=\\rho(w)e_s\\in\\Phi_I^+$ with $w\\in W_I$, $s\\in I$, then $t_\\alpha=wsw^{-1}\\in W_I$; conversely, if $t\\in W_I\\cap T$, choose a reduced expression $t=s_1\\cdots s_k$ of $t$ inside $W_I$ (its letters lie in $I$ by [[thm-hh-parabolic-minimal-representatives-and-length-additivity]] (1)) and apply strong exchange [[thm-cg-root-inversion-formulas-and-strong-exchange]] (3) to $t\\cdot t=1$: this exhibits $t=s_1\\cdots s_{i-1}s_is_{i-1}\\cdots s_1$ as a conjugate of a letter $s_i\\in I$, so its root lies in $\\Phi_I$ up to sign, and $\\Phi_I$ is stable under negation because $-\\rho(u)e_s=\\rho(us)e_s$. This proves the reflection identification and completes (2).\n**(3)** Let $x\\in wW_I=dW_I$. By [[thm-hh-parabolic-minimal-representatives-and-length-additivity]] (3) (the right-coset clause), $x=dv$ for a unique $v\\in W_I$ and $\\ell(x)=\\ell(d)+\\ell(v)$. Hence $\\ell(x)\\ge\\ell(d)$, with equality precisely when $\\ell(v)=0$, i.e. $v=1$, i.e. $x=d$. This gives the first displayed description. For the two descriptions: if $d\\in W^I$ then $\\ell(dv)=\\ell(d)+\\ell(v)\\ge\\ell(d)$ for all $v\\in W_I$ by the same additivity; conversely, if $\\ell(d)\\le\\ell(dv)$ for all $v\\in W_I$, take $v=s\\in I$: then $\\ell(ds)\\ge\\ell(d)$ and $\\ell(ds)\\neq\\ell(d)$ by the parity statement of [[thm-hh-coxeter-exchange-deletion-and-faithfulness]] (1), so $\\ell(ds)>\\ell(d)$ and $d\\in W^I$. The left-handed assertions follow by inversion, using $\\ell(x)=\\ell(x^{-1})$ and the same additivity statement for $W_I$ applied to $d^{-1}$. Finally, each $wW_I$ contains an element of $W^I$ (the first factor $d$ in the factorization $w=dv$ of $w$), and it is unique there by the equality case just proved, hence it is the unique element of minimum length. No use of the Axiom of Choice is made.",
    "sources": {
     "references": [
      {
       "title": "Anders Bjorner and Francesco Brenti, Combinatorics of Coxeter Groups (Graduate Texts in Mathematics 231, Springer 2005; author-hosted complete PDF)",
       "url": "https://sites.math.washington.edu/~billey/classes/reflection.groups/references/EntireBook.pdf",
       "locator": "Section 2.4, printed pp. 38-41: Proposition 2.4.1(i)-(iii) for the intrinsic Coxeter structure and the intersection, Proposition 2.4.4 and Corollary 2.4.5(i) for the unique length-additive factorization and the minimal coset representatives."
      },
      {
       "title": "Dongwen Qi, A Note on Parabolic Subgroups of a Coxeter Group (arXiv:math/0512408)",
       "url": "https://arxiv.org/pdf/math/0512408",
       "locator": "Section 3, Lemma 3.1 with its complete proof (pp. 4-5 of the arXiv version): Phi_I = Phi cap span{alpha_s : s in I}, by induction on the length of the reflection with root phi, using the root-sign criterion and the length criterion of Section 2 (Theorem 2.1 and Proposition 2.2, p. 3)."
      }
     ]
