   {
    "id": "lem-cg-double-coset-descent-reduction-and-minimality",
    "kind": "lemma",
    "title": "Descent reduction, minimum-length elements, and the additive factorization in a double coset",
    "deps": [
     "def-cg-parabolic-quotient-and-two-sided-minima",
     "def-hh-coxeter-matrix-word-group-and-length",
     "thm-hh-coxeter-exchange-deletion-and-faithfulness",
     "thm-hh-parabolic-minimal-representatives-and-length-additivity",
     "thm-well-ordering-principle",
     "def-natural-numbers",
     "def-group"
    ],
    "statement": "Let $(S,m)$, $W$, $\\ell$ be as in [[def-cg-parabolic-quotient-and-two-sided-minima]], let $I,J\\subseteq S$, and let $W_I$, $W^J$, ${}^IW$, ${}^IW^J$ be as defined there; for $d\\in W$ write\n$$\\Omega(d):=W_IdW_J=\\{udv:u\\in W_I,\\ v\\in W_J\\}$$\nfor the double coset of $d$.\n\n**(1) Descent reduction.** Every set $\\Omega(d)$ contains an element of ${}^IW^J$. More precisely, starting from $x:=d$ and repeatedly replacing the current element $x$ by $sx$ whenever $s\\in I$ satisfies $\\ell(sx)<\\ell(x)$, and by $xs$ whenever $s\\in J$ satisfies $\\ell(xs)<\\ell(x)$, the process stops after at most $\\ell(d)$ replacements at an element of $\\Omega(d)\\cap{}^IW^J$.\n\n**(2) Elements of ${}^IW^J$ have minimum length.** Let $d\\in{}^IW^J$. Then\n$$\\ell(d)\\le\\ell(x)\\quad\\text{for every }x\\in\\Omega(d),\\qquad\\text{and}\\qquad \\ell(x)=\\ell(d)\\iff x=d.$$\nConsequently every double coset $W_IwW_J$ contains exactly one element of ${}^IW^J$, and it is the unique element of minimum length of that double coset; conversely, every element of minimum length in its double coset $W_IxW_J$ lies in ${}^IW^J$.\n\n**(3) Additive factorization.** Let $d\\in{}^IW^J$ and $x\\in\\Omega(d)$. Then there exist $u\\in W_I$ and $v\\in W_J$ with\n$$x=udv,\\qquad \\ell(x)=\\ell(u)+\\ell(d)+\\ell(v).$$",
    "strategy": "**(1)** Each replacement keeps the element inside the same double coset: replacing $x$ by $sx$ with $s\\in I$ multiplies on the left by an element of the subgroup $W_I$, and replacing $x$ by $xs$ with $s\\in J$ multiplies on the right by an element of $W_J$ ([[def-group]]). Each step lowers $\\ell$ by exactly $1$, by the parity statement $\\ell(sx)=\\ell(x)\\pm1$, $\\ell(xs)=\\ell(x)\\pm1$ of [[thm-hh-coxeter-exchange-deletion-and-faithfulness]] (1); since $\\ell$ takes values in the natural numbers ([[def-natural-numbers]]), only finitely many steps are possible, at most $\\ell(d)$ of them. When no step is possible, the current element $x$ satisfies $\\ell(sx)>\\ell(x)$ for all $s\\in I$ and $\\ell(xs)>\\ell(x)$ for all $s\\in J$, i.e. $x\\in{}^IW^J$. This proves (1).\n**(2)** A minimum-length element of $\\Omega:=W_IwW_J$ exists because $\\{\\ell(x):x\\in\\Omega\\}\\subseteq\\mathbb N$ is nonempty and has a least element ([[thm-well-ordering-principle]]). Let $m$ be such an element. If $s\\in I$ satisfied $\\ell(sm)<\\ell(m)$, then $sm\\in\\Omega$ (since $s\\in W_I$) would be shorter, a contradiction; and similarly $\\ell(ms)>\\ell(m)$ for all $s\\in J$. Hence $m\\in{}^IW^J$, and together with (1) this shows that each double coset contains at least one element of ${}^IW^J$.\nWe next prove the additive factorization (3) for such a minimum $m$, following Davis's proof of Lemma 4.3.1. Write an arbitrary $x\\in\\Omega$ as $x=ama'$ with $a\\in W_I$, $a'\\in W_J$, choose reduced expressions $A$ for $a$, $M$ for $m$ and $A'$ for $a'$, and consider the word $A\\,M\\,A'$, which represents $x$. By the deletion process of [[thm-hh-coxeter-exchange-deletion-and-faithfulness]] (3), while the current word is not reduced one may delete a pair of positions carrying equal letters so that the resulting word represents the same element $x$. We claim that no deletion removes a letter of the middle block $M$. If both deleted letters lay in $M$, then $M$ with two letters deleted would represent the same element as $M$, contradicting reducedness of $M$; the equality of the two words' values is cancelled using the outer factors in the group ([[def-group]]). If one deleted letter lay in $M$ and the other in $A$, the resulting word represents $x$ and has the form $A_1M_1A'$ with $A_1$ equal to $A$ with one letter deleted and $M_1$ equal to $M$ with one letter deleted; then $M_1=a_1^{-1}x(a')^{-1}$, where $a_1$ is the value of $A_1$ and lies in $W_I$, so $M_1\\in W_IxW_J=\\Omega$ while $\\ell(M_1)\\le\\ell(m)-1<\\ell(m)$, contradicting the minimality of $m$; the case 'one letter in $M$ and one in $A'$' is symmetric. If both deleted letters lay in $A$ (or both in $A'$), then $A$ with two letters deleted would represent the same element as $A$, contradicting reducedness of $A$. Hence every deletion removes one letter from $A$ and one from $A'$, the process terminates, and it produces a reduced word of $x$ of the form $A_{\\mathrm{red}}\\,M\\,A'_{\\mathrm{red}}$, where $A_{\\mathrm{red}}$, $A'_{\\mathrm{red}}$ arise from $A$, $A'$ by deletions. Therefore\n$$x=a_0\\,m\\,a_0'\\quad\\text{with }a_0\\in W_I,\\ a_0'\\in W_J,\\qquad \\ell(x)=\\ell(a_0)+\\ell(m)+\\ell(a_0'),$$\nwhich is (3) for the minimum $m$.\nNow let $d\\in{}^IW^J$ be arbitrary and apply this with $x:=d$ in $\\Omega(d)$: $d=a_0ma_0'$ with $\\ell(d)=\\ell(a_0)+\\ell(m)+\\ell(a_0')$. If $a_0\\neq1$, choose a left descent $s\\in I$ of $a_0$ (a reduced expression of $a_0$ inside $W_I$ has all its letters in $I$ and starts with such an $s$, [[thm-hh-parabolic-minimal-representatives-and-length-additivity]] (1)); then, by subadditivity of $\\ell$ (immediate from the definition of $\\ell$ as a minimal word length in [[def-hh-coxeter-matrix-word-group-and-length]]: concatenating reduced expressions gives $\\ell(uv)\\le\\ell(u)+\\ell(v)$),\n$$\\ell(sd)=\\ell(sa_0ma_0')\\le\\ell(sa_0)+\\ell(m)+\\ell(a_0')=\\ell(a_0)-1+\\ell(m)+\\ell(a_0')=\\ell(d)-1<\\ell(d),$$\ncontradicting $d\\in{}^IW$, which forces $\\ell(sd)=\\ell(d)+1$; hence $a_0=1$, and symmetrically $a_0'=1$, so $d=m$. Thus every element of ${}^IW^J$ is a minimum-length element of its double coset, and every minimum-length element lies in ${}^IW^J$. The remaining clauses of (2) follow from the equality case in (3): if $x\\in\\Omega$ and $d\\in{}^IW^J\\cap\\Omega$, then $x=a_0da_0'$ additively, so $\\ell(x)\\ge\\ell(d)$ with equality if and only if $a_0=a_0'=1$, i.e. $x=d$; in particular a double coset contains at most one element of ${}^IW^J$, and it is its unique element of minimum length. No use of the Axiom of Choice is made: each deletion is determined by the current word, and the minimum-length element is chosen from a set of natural numbers.",
    "sources": {
     "references": [
      {
       "title": "Michael W. Davis, The Geometry and Topology of Coxeter Groups (first-edition author manuscript, 2007-2008)",
       "url": "https://people.math.osu.edu/davis.12/davisbook.pdf",
       "locator": "Section 4.3, printed pp. 47-48: Lemma 4.3.1 with its proof (existence and uniqueness of the shortest element of a double coset and the additive factorization w'=awa'), and Definition 4.3.2 of (T,T')-reduced elements; the middle-block argument is Davis's deletion argument."
      },
      {
       "title": "Anders Bjorner and Francesco Brenti, Combinatorics of Coxeter Groups (Graduate Texts in Mathematics 231, Springer 2005; author-hosted complete PDF)",
       "url": "https://sites.math.washington.edu/~billey/classes/reflection.groups/references/EntireBook.pdf",
       "locator": "Exercise 15(a)-(b), printed p. 58, with Section 2.4, printed pp. 38-41: unique minimal element of a double coset and its characterization via the quotients W^I, W^J."
      },
      {
       "title": "George Lusztig, Hecke Algebras with Unequal Parameters (revised arXiv edition of the CRM monograph, arXiv:math/0208154v2)",
       "url": "https://arxiv.org/pdf/math/0208154v2",
       "locator": "Chapter 9, the first part of the proof of Proposition 9.15 in 9.16(a)-(b) (pp. 44-46): minimality of b keeps the middle block of a reduced expression intact under deletion, and every element of the double coset has an additive factorization a b c."
      },
      {
       "title": "Sara Billey, Matjaz Konvalinka, T. Kyle Petersen, William Slofstra and Bridget Tenner, Parabolic double cosets in Coxeter groups (arXiv:1612.00736v2)",
       "url": "https://arxiv.org/pdf/1612.00736v2",
       "locator": "Proposition 2.7(a),(b) (printed p. 7 of the arXiv version): every parabolic double coset has a unique minimal element, and the minimal elements are exactly the elements of ^IW^J."
      }
     ]
    },
    "provenance": {
     "statement": "literature-derived",
     "proof": "ai-altered"
    },
    "dependency_level": 7
   },
   {
    "id": "lem-cg-double-coset-intersection-parabolic",
    "kind": "lemma",
    "title": "The parabolic intersection W_I cap dW_Jd inverse for d in ^IW^J",
    "deps": [
     "def-cg-parabolic-quotient-and-two-sided-minima",
     "thm-cg-parabolic-intersections-and-coset-factorization",
     "lem-cg-double-coset-descent-reduction-and-minimality",
     "def-hh-coxeter-matrix-word-group-and-length",
     "thm-hh-coxeter-exchange-deletion-and-faithfulness",
     "thm-hh-parabolic-minimal-representatives-and-length-additivity",
     "def-cg-canonical-reflection-homomorphism",
     "thm-cg-root-length-criterion-and-faithfulness",
     "thm-cg-root-inversion-formulas-and-strong-exchange",
     "def-group"
    ],
    "statement": "Let $(S,m)$, $W$, $\\ell$ be as in [[def-cg-parabolic-quotient-and-two-sided-minima]], let $I,J\\subseteq S$, and let $d\\in{}^IW^J$. Put\n$$K:=I\\cap dJd^{-1}=\\{s\\in I:d^{-1}sd\\in J\\},$$\nwhere $dJd^{-1}=\\{djd^{-1}:j\\in J\\}\\subseteq T$. Let $V_I$, $\\Phi_I$ be as in [[thm-cg-parabolic-intersections-and-coset-factorization]] (2) and let $\\alpha\\mapsto t_\\alpha$ be the root-reflection dictionary of [[thm-cg-root-inversion-formulas-and-strong-exchange]] (1).\n\n**(1) The intersection.** $W_I\\cap dW_Jd^{-1}=W_K$. Conjugating by $d$ gives\n$$d^{-1}W_Kd=W_{d^{-1}Kd},\\qquad d^{-1}Kd=\\{d^{-1}sd:s\\in K\\}\\subseteq J,$$\nand equivalently $W_J\\cap d^{-1}W_Id=W_{d^{-1}Kd}$.\n\n**(2) The descent form.** If $y\\in W_I\\cap dW_Jd^{-1}$ and $y=s_1\\cdots s_p$ is a reduced expression of $y$ with letters $s_1,\\dots,s_p\\in I$, then\n$$d^{-1}s_id\\in J\\qquad\\text{for every }i=1,\\dots,p.$$\n\n**(3) The conjugated positive root is simple.** Let $s\\in I$. Then $z:=d^{-1}sd$ is a reflection of $W$ (that is, $z\\in T$), its root is $\\rho(d)^{-1}e_s\\in\\Phi_+$, and $z\\in J$ if and only if this root is one of the simple roots $e_j$, $j\\in J$. Moreover, if $z\\in W_J$ then already $z\\in J$: a reflection of the form $d^{-1}sd$ with $s\\in I$ that lies in $W_J$ is necessarily a *simple* reflection of the parabolic root system $\\Phi_J=\\Phi\\cap V_J$ of [[thm-cg-parabolic-intersections-and-coset-factorization]] (2), never a non-simple positive combination such as $e_j+e_{j'}$.",
    "strategy": "**Setup.** By [[lem-cg-double-coset-descent-reduction-and-minimality]] (2), $d$ has minimum length in $\\Omega:=W_IdW_J$; by the definition of ${}^IW^J$ we have $\\ell(sd)>\\ell(d)$ for $s\\in I$ and $\\ell(ds)>\\ell(d)$ for $s\\in J$, and since these differences lie in $\\{\\pm1\\}$ ([[thm-hh-coxeter-exchange-deletion-and-faithfulness]] (1)) they equal $+1$. Hence the length-additivity statements of [[thm-hh-parabolic-minimal-representatives-and-length-additivity]] (3) give\n$$\\ell(ud)=\\ell(u)+\\ell(d)\\ \\ (u\\in W_I),\\qquad \\ell(dv)=\\ell(d)+\\ell(v)\\ \\ (v\\in W_J).$$\nThe inclusion $W_K\\subseteq W_I\\cap dW_Jd^{-1}$ is immediate: if $s\\in K$ then $s\\in I\\subseteq W_I$ and $d^{-1}sd\\in J$, so $s=d(d^{-1}sd)d^{-1}\\in dW_Jd^{-1}$.\n**The key step.** Let $y\\in W_I\\cap dW_Jd^{-1}$ and let $y=s_1\\cdots s_p$ be a reduced expression of $y$ in $W_I$ (its letters lie in $I$ by [[thm-hh-parabolic-minimal-representatives-and-length-additivity]] (1)); put $\\tilde y:=d^{-1}yd\\in W_J$. Then $\\ell(y)=\\ell(\\tilde y)$: indeed $yd=d\\tilde y$, while $\\ell(yd)=\\ell(y)+\\ell(d)$ and $\\ell(d\\tilde y)=\\ell(d)+\\ell(\\tilde y)$ by the two additivity statements above. Fix a reduced expression $d=a_1\\cdots a_q$ and write $\\tilde y=\\tilde y_1\\cdots\\tilde y_p$; then $a_1\\cdots a_q\\tilde y_1\\cdots\\tilde y_p$ and $s_1\\cdots s_pa_1\\cdots a_q$ are both reduced expressions of $yd=d\\tilde y$, of length $\\ell(d)+\\ell(y)$. The element $s_1$ is a left descent of $yd$: $s_1y=s_2\\cdots s_p$ has length $p-1$, so $\\ell(s_1\\cdot yd)=\\ell(s_1y)+\\ell(d)=\\ell(yd)-1$. Applying strong exchange [[thm-cg-root-inversion-formulas-and-strong-exchange]] (3) to the reduced expression $a_1\\cdots a_q\\tilde y_1\\cdots\\tilde y_p$ of $yd$ and the reflection $s_1\\in T$ gives a unique index $i\\in\\{1,\\dots,q+p\\}$ such that $s_1(yd)$ is the value of this word with its $i$-th letter deleted and\n$$s_1=a_1\\cdots a_{i-1}a_ia_{i-1}\\cdots a_1\\quad(i\\le q),\\qquad s_1=X\\tilde y_jX^{-1}\\quad(i=q+j,\\ X:=d\\,\\tilde y_1\\cdots\\tilde y_{j-1}).$$\nIn the first case $s_1a_1\\cdots a_{i-1}=a_1\\cdots a_{i-1}a_i$, and multiplying the identity $s_1(yd)=s_2\\cdots s_p\\cdot d$ on the right by $(\\tilde y_1\\cdots\\tilde y_p)^{-1}$ exhibits the element $d_i:=a_1\\cdots a_{i-1}a_{i+1}\\cdots a_q$ as\n$$d_i=(s_2\\cdots s_p)\\,d\\,(\\tilde y_1\\cdots\\tilde y_p)^{-1}\\in W_IdW_J=\\Omega;$$\nbut $d_i$ is the value of a word of length $q-1$, so $\\ell(d_i)\\le q-1<q=\\ell(d)$, contradicting the minimality of $d$ in $\\Omega$. Hence only the second case occurs, and it yields\n$$d^{-1}s_1d=\\tilde y_1\\cdots\\tilde y_{j-1}\\tilde y_j\\tilde y_{j-1}\\cdots\\tilde y_1\\in W_J,$$\na conjugate in $W_J$ of the letter $\\tilde y_j\\in J$. Finally $d^{-1}y'd=(d^{-1}s_1d)^{-1}(d^{-1}yd)\\in W_J$ for $y':=s_2\\cdots s_p\\in W_I$; induction on $p$ therefore gives $d^{-1}s_id\\in W_J$ for every $i$.\n**Length one forces simplicity.** Let $s\\in I$ and suppose $z:=d^{-1}sd\\in W_J$. Repeating the length computation of the key step with $(y,\\tilde y)$ replaced by $(s,z)$ is legitimate because $s\\in W_I$, $z\\in W_J$ and $sd=dz$; it gives $\\ell(z)=\\ell(s)=1$. An element of $W_J$ of length one is a simple reflection, since its support is a singleton ([[thm-hh-parabolic-minimal-representatives-and-length-additivity]] (1)) and $W_J\\cap S=J$ ([[thm-hh-parabolic-minimal-representatives-and-length-additivity]] (2)); hence $z\\in J$. Applying this to each letter $s_1,\\dots,s_p$ above, every such letter lies in $K$, so every $y\\in W_I\\cap dW_Jd^{-1}$ lies in $W_K$. Together with the easy inclusion this is (1); (2) is exactly the statement just proved, and conjugating $W_I\\cap dW_Jd^{-1}=W_K$ by $d$ gives $W_J\\cap d^{-1}W_Id=W_{d^{-1}Kd}$ with $d^{-1}Kd\\subseteq J$.\n**The root form (3).** By the dictionary [[thm-cg-root-inversion-formulas-and-strong-exchange]] (1), $z=d^{-1}sd$ is the reflection $t_{\\rho(d)^{-1}e_s}$, and its root lies in $\\Phi_+$: indeed $d\\in{}^IW$ gives $\\ell(sd)>\\ell(d)$, which by the root-length criterion [[thm-cg-root-length-criterion-and-faithfulness]] (1) applied to $d^{-1}$ (using $\\ell(sd)=\\ell(d^{-1}s)$ and $\\rho(d^{-1})=\\rho(d)^{-1}$) says exactly $\\rho(d)^{-1}e_s\\in\\Phi_+$. Now use the parabolic root system of [[thm-cg-parabolic-intersections-and-coset-factorization]] (2) with $I$ replaced by $J$: $\\Phi_J=\\Phi\\cap V_J$ is a root system whose simple roots are precisely $e_j$, $j\\in J$. If $z\\in W_J$ then, as shown, $z\\in J$; equivalently, by the dictionary (1)(iii)-(iv), the conjugated positive root $\\rho(d)^{-1}e_s$ equals $e_j$ for some $j\\in J$. Thus membership of the conjugated reflection in $W_J$ forces the conjugated root to be *simple* in $\\Phi_J$: an element such as $t_{e_j+e_{j'}}$ with distinct $j,j'\\in J$ is a reflection of $W_J$ of length $>1$ and is excluded by $\\ell(z)=1$. This proves (3). No use of the Axiom of Choice is made.",
    "sources": {
     "references": [
      {
       "title": "George Lusztig, Hecke Algebras with Unequal Parameters (revised arXiv edition of the CRM monograph, arXiv:math/0208154v2)",
       "url": "https://arxiv.org/pdf/math/0208154v2",
       "locator": "Chapter 9, Proposition 9.15(b) and its proof in 9.16(c),(d) (pp. 44-46): 9.16(c) is the key step (every b^{-1}s'_ib lies in K') and 9.16(d) shows that a conjugated simple reflection that lies in W_{K'} is already in K'."
      },
      {
       "title": "Sara Billey, Matjaz Konvalinka, T. Kyle Petersen, William Slofstra and Bridget Tenner, Parabolic double cosets in Coxeter groups (arXiv:1612.00736v2)",
       "url": "https://arxiv.org/pdf/1612.00736v2",
       "locator": "Corollary 2.8 with Proposition 2.7 (printed pp. 7-8 of the arXiv version): the intersection is written H=I cap (wJw^{-1}) and u runs over the transversal W_I^H."
      },
      {
       "title": "Dongwen Qi, A Note on Parabolic Subgroups of a Coxeter Group (arXiv:math/0512408)",
       "url": "https://arxiv.org/pdf/math/0512408",
       "locator": "Section 3, p. 5: the basic fact used in the proof of Lemma 3.2 — if x t x^{-1} lies in a special subgroup W_K with t in S, then it is a reflection of W_K."
      }
     ]
    },
    "provenance": {
     "statement": "literature-derived",
     "proof": "ai-altered"
    },
    "dependency_level": 13
   },
   {
    "id": "thm-cg-double-coset-unique-minimum-and-normal-form",
    "kind": "theorem",
    "title": "Unique minimal double coset representatives and the additive normal form u-d-v",
    "deps": [
     "def-cg-parabolic-quotient-and-two-sided-minima",
     "lem-cg-double-coset-descent-reduction-and-minimality",
     "lem-cg-double-coset-intersection-parabolic",
     "def-hh-coxeter-matrix-word-group-and-length",
     "thm-hh-parabolic-minimal-representatives-and-length-additivity",
     "def-group"
    ],
    "statement": "Let $I,J\\subseteq S$, let $d\\in{}^IW^J$, and put\n$$K:=I\\cap dJd^{-1}=\\{s\\in I:d^{-1}sd\\in J\\},$$\nso that $W_I\\cap dW_Jd^{-1}=W_K$ by [[lem-cg-double-coset-intersection-parabolic]]. Write\n$$W_I^K:=\\{u\\in W_I:\\ell(us)>\\ell(u)\\text{ for all }s\\in K\\}$$\nfor the set of elements of $W_I$ with no right descent in $K$ — the same construction as in [[def-cg-parabolic-quotient-and-two-sided-minima]] (2), applied inside the Coxeter system $(W_I,I)$ with its standard parabolic subgroup $W_K$.\n\n**(1) Unique minimum.** Two elements of ${}^IW^J$ lie in a common double coset $W_IwW_J$ only if they are equal. Hence, by [[lem-cg-double-coset-descent-reduction-and-minimality]] (1),(2), every double coset $W_IwW_J$ contains exactly one element of ${}^IW^J$, namely its unique element of minimum length; in particular $d$ is uniquely determined by the double coset $W_IdW_J$.\n\n**(2) The additive normal form.** Every $x\\in W_IdW_J$ has a unique representation\n$$x=u\\,d\\,v\\qquad\\text{with }u\\in W_I^K,\\ v\\in W_J,$$\nand every product $udv$ with $u\\in W_I^K$, $v\\in W_J$ lies in $W_IdW_J$. Thus\n$$W_I^K\\times W_J\\to W_IdW_J,\\qquad (u,v)\\mapsto udv$$\nis a bijection, and for all $u\\in W_I^K$ and $v\\in W_J$\n$$\\ell(udv)=\\ell(u)+\\ell(d)+\\ell(v).$$\n\n**(3) The size of a double coset.** $|W_IdW_J|=|W_I^K|\\cdot|W_J|$; and if $W_I$ is finite then $|W_IdW_J|=|W_I|\\,|W_J|/|W_K|$.\n\n**(4) The restriction to $W_I^K$ is necessary.** For arbitrary $u\\in W_I$ the representation $x=udv$ is not unique in general: if $u=u_0z$ is the factorization of $u$ with $u_0\\in W_I^K$ and $z\\in W_K$, and $z':=d^{-1}zd\\in W_J$, then\n$$udv=u_0\\,d\\,(z'v),\\qquad u_0\\in W_I^K,\\ z'v\\in W_J,$$\nand $z'\\neq1$ whenever $z\\neq1$; so the same $x$ has two representations of the form $udv$ with different first factors unless $u\\in W_I^K$ already. This is why the transversal restriction in (2) is recorded explicitly and may not be dropped.",
    "strategy": "**Ingredients.** By [[thm-hh-parabolic-minimal-representatives-and-length-additivity]] (3) applied to the Coxeter system $(W_I,I)$, every $u\\in W_I$ has a unique factorization $u=u_0z$ with $u_0\\in W_I^K$, $z\\in W_K$, and then $\\ell(u)=\\ell(u_0)+\\ell(z)$; by [[lem-cg-double-coset-intersection-parabolic]] (1), $W_K=W_I\\cap dW_Jd^{-1}$ and $d^{-1}W_Kd\\subseteq W_J$. By [[lem-cg-double-coset-descent-reduction-and-minimality]] (2),(3), $d$ is a minimum-length element of $\\Omega:=W_IdW_J$ and every $x\\in\\Omega$ admits an additive factorization $x=a\\,d\\,c$ with $a\\in W_I$, $c\\in W_J$ and $\\ell(x)=\\ell(a)+\\ell(d)+\\ell(c)$.\n**Existence.** Given $x\\in\\Omega$, choose such $a,c$. Factor $a=u_0z$ with $u_0\\in W_I^K$, $z\\in W_K$ and $\\ell(a)=\\ell(u_0)+\\ell(z)$, put $z':=d^{-1}zd\\in W_J$ and $v:=z'c\\in W_J$. Using $zd=dz'$,\n$$x=a\\,d\\,c=u_0\\,z\\,d\\,c=u_0\\,d\\,z'\\,c=u_0\\,d\\,v$$\nwith $u_0\\in W_I^K$, $v\\in W_J$: the map is surjective.\n**Additivity.** With this notation, $\\ell(z)=\\ell(z')$: indeed $zd=dz'$, while $\\ell(zd)=\\ell(z)+\\ell(d)$ (the additivity statement of [[thm-hh-parabolic-minimal-representatives-and-length-additivity]] (3) applied to $z\\in W_I$, $d\\in{}^IW$) and $\\ell(dz')=\\ell(d)+\\ell(z')$ (applied to $d\\in W^J$, $z'\\in W_J$). Estimating $x=u_0dv$ and using subadditivity of $\\ell$ (immediate from the definition of $\\ell$ as a minimal word length in [[def-hh-coxeter-matrix-word-group-and-length]]: concatenate reduced expressions to obtain $\\ell(\\alpha\\beta)\\le\\ell(\\alpha)+\\ell(\\beta)$),\n$$\\ell(x)\\le\\ell(u_0)+\\ell(d)+\\ell(v)\\le\\ell(u_0)+\\ell(d)+\\ell(z')+\\ell(c)=\\ell(u_0)+\\ell(z)+\\ell(d)+\\ell(c)=\\ell(x),$$\nbecause $\\ell(x)=\\ell(a)+\\ell(d)+\\ell(c)$ and $\\ell(a)=\\ell(u_0)+\\ell(z)$. Hence all inequalities are equalities; in particular $\\ell(udv)=\\ell(u)+\\ell(d)+\\ell(v)$ for $u:=u_0$, $v=z'c$, and also $\\ell(v)=\\ell(z')+\\ell(c)$.\n**Injectivity.** Suppose $udv=u'dv'$ with $u,u'\\in W_I^K$, $v,v'\\in W_J$. Manipulating in the group $W$ ([[def-group]]),\n$$u^{-1}u'=d\\,v\\,v'^{-1}d^{-1}\\in W_I\\cap dW_Jd^{-1}=W_K$$\nby [[lem-cg-double-coset-intersection-parabolic]] (1), so $u'$ lies in the coset $uW_K$; since $u$ and $u'$ are both minimal-length representatives of that coset in $W_I$ ([[thm-hh-parabolic-minimal-representatives-and-length-additivity]] (3) inside $W_I$), $u=u'$, and then $v=v'$. This proves the bijection in (2); (3) is immediate from it, and $|W_I^K|=|W_I|/|W_K|$ when $W_I$ is finite because $W_I^K\\times W_K\\to W_I$, $(u_0,z)\\mapsto u_0z$, is a bijection by the same factorization statement.\n**(1)** Existence and minimality of the element of ${}^IW^J$ in each double coset are [[lem-cg-double-coset-descent-reduction-and-minimality]] (1),(2). If $d,d'\\in{}^IW^J$ lie in the same double coset, then by (2) applied to $x:=d'$ with the base point $d$ we have $d'=udv$ with $u\\in W_I^K$, $v\\in W_J$ and $\\ell(d')=\\ell(u)+\\ell(d)+\\ell(v)\\ge\\ell(d)$; but both $d$ and $d'$ are minimum-length elements of the same double coset by [[lem-cg-double-coset-descent-reduction-and-minimality]] (2), so $\\ell(d')=\\ell(d)$ and the equality case forces $u=v=1$, i.e. $d'=d$. Hence the element of ${}^IW^J$ in a double coset is unique, and (1) follows.\n**(4)** The factorization $u=u_0z$ with $u_0\\in W_I^K$, $z\\in W_K$ is the one from $W_I^K\\times W_K\\to W_I$; $z'=d^{-1}zd\\in W_J$ by [[lem-cg-double-coset-intersection-parabolic]] (1); and $z'=1$ if and only if $z=1$. Substituting $u=u_0z$ and $zd=dz'$ gives $udv=u_0d(z'v)$ with $u_0\\in W_I^K$ and $z'v\\in W_J$, so the representation is not unique whenever $z\\neq1$. No use of the Axiom of Choice is made.",
    "sources": {
     "references": [
      {
       "title": "George Lusztig, Hecke Algebras with Unequal Parameters (revised arXiv edition of the CRM monograph, arXiv:math/0208154v2)",
       "url": "https://arxiv.org/pdf/math/0208154v2",
       "locator": "Chapter 9, Proposition 9.15(c),(d) and the proof in 9.16(e),(f),(g) (pp. 44-46): existence of the additive factorization a b c with a in W_K^J, injectivity, and length additivity, with 9.15(b) as the intersection input."
      },
      {
       "title": "Sara Billey, Matjaz Konvalinka, T. Kyle Petersen, William Slofstra and Bridget Tenner, Parabolic double cosets in Coxeter groups (arXiv:1612.00736v2)",
       "url": "https://arxiv.org/pdf/1612.00736v2",
       "locator": "Corollary 2.8 (double parabolic decomposition) with the transversal W_I^H, H=I cap wJw^{-1} (printed pp. 7-8 of the arXiv version), and Proposition 2.7(a),(b) for the unique minimal element."
      },
      {
       "title": "Michael W. Davis, The Geometry and Topology of Coxeter Groups (first-edition author manuscript, 2007-2008)",
       "url": "https://people.math.osu.edu/davis.12/davisbook.pdf",
       "locator": "Lemma 4.3.1, printed pp. 47-48: additive factorization in a double coset with respect to its shortest element, and Definition 4.3.2 of (T,T')-reduced elements."
      },
      {
       "title": "Anders Bjorner and Francesco Brenti, Combinatorics of Coxeter Groups (Graduate Texts in Mathematics 231, Springer 2005; author-hosted complete PDF)",
       "url": "https://sites.math.washington.edu/~billey/classes/reflection.groups/references/EntireBook.pdf",
       "locator": "Proposition 2.4.4 and its corollaries, printed pp. 39-41, for the factorization inside W_I used in the existence step; Exercise 15(a), printed p. 58."
