    ],
    "statement": "**(i) Two conjugate reflections generating a parabolic subgroup that is not standard.** Let $W=S_4$ with simple reflections $s_1=(1\\,2)$, $s_2=(2\\,3)$, $s_3=(3\\,4)$ ([[def-hh-coxeter-matrix-word-group-and-length]]), and let\n$$H:=\\langle (1\\,3),\\,(2\\,4)\\rangle\\le S_4.$$\nWriting one-line notation, $(1\\,3)=3214$ and $(2\\,4)=1432$, and\n$$H=\\{1234,\\ 1432,\\ 3214,\\ 3412\\}$$\nhas order $4$; moreover $(1\\,3)=s_2s_1s_2$, $(2\\,4)=s_2s_3s_2$, so\n$$H=s_2\\,W_{\\{s_1,s_3\\}}\\,s_2,$$\ni.e. $H$ is the conjugate of a standard parabolic subgroup. The two generators are conjugate in $S_4$: indeed $(2\\,4)=w(1\\,3)w^{-1}$ for $w=(1\\,2)(3\\,4)=2143$. Finally $H$ is not equal to any of the eight standard parabolic subgroups $W_I$ ($I\\subseteq\\{s_1,s_2,s_3\\}$) of $S_4$. Thus $H$ is a parabolic subgroup (a conjugate of a standard one) which is not standard, generated by two conjugate reflections: 'standard parabolic' is strictly stronger than 'parabolic'.\n\n**(ii) A reflection subgroup that is not parabolic.** Let $W=\\langle s,t\\rangle$ be the infinite dihedral group with $m(s,t)=\\infty$ and put $u:=st$ ([[lem-hh-dihedral-root-recurrence-and-root-sign]] (3)(c), (4)). The element $tst$ is a reflection (a conjugate of $s$), and\n$$H':=\\langle s,\\ tst\\rangle=\\langle s,\\ u^2\\rangle=\\{u^{2k}:k\\in\\mathbb Z\\}\\cup\\{u^{2k}s:k\\in\\mathbb Z\\}\\le W$$\nis a subgroup generated by the two reflections $s$ and $tst$, i.e. a reflection subgroup. It contains $u^2$ and $u^{-2}$, has index $2$ in $W$, and is infinite; consequently it is not equal to $W$ and not one of the order-two subgroups $\\{1,r\\}$, and the parabolic subgroups of $W$ are exactly $\\{1\\}$, the order-two subgroups $\\{1,r\\}$ generated by a reflection $r\\in T$, and $W$ itself, so that $H'$ is a reflection subgroup which is *not* a parabolic subgroup. Together with (i) this shows that the three classes\n$$\\text{standard parabolic}\\ \\subsetneq\\ \\text{parabolic}\\ \\subsetneq\\ \\text{reflection subgroup}$$\nare pairwise distinct, where the first inclusion is the definition and the others are the two computations above.",
    "strategy": "**(i)** Direct computation: the two generators are involutions, their product $(1\\,3)(2\\,4)$ is an involution, so the generated subgroup is $H=\\{1,(1\\,3),(2\\,4),(1\\,3)(2\\,4)\\}$ of order $4$; in one-line notation these are $1234,3214,1432,3412$ because $(1\\,3)(2\\,4)$ maps $1\\mapsto3,2\\mapsto4,3\\mapsto1,4\\mapsto2$. The identities $s_2s_1s_2=(1\\,3)$ and $s_2s_3s_2=(2\\,4)$ are checked by composing one-line permutations (or by the reflection formula of [[def-cg-real-coxeter-form-and-reflection]] (3)), and conjugating the standard parabolic $W_{\\{s_1,s_3\\}}=\\langle s_1,s_3\\rangle=\\{1234,2134,1243,2143\\}$ by $s_2$ gives $\\langle s_2s_1s_2,\\,s_2s_3s_2\\rangle=H$. The conjugating relation $(2\\,4)=w(1\\,3)w^{-1}$ for $w=2143=(1\\,2)(3\\,4)$ is the standard formula for conjugation of a transposition. The eight standard parabolic subgroups of $S_4$ are the $W_I$ for the eight subsets $I\\subseteq\\{s_1,s_2,s_3\\}$, and they are pairwise distinct because $W_I\\cap S=I$ ([[thm-hh-parabolic-minimal-representatives-and-length-additivity]] (2)); comparing their element lists with $H$ shows $H\\neq W_I$ for every $I$.\n**(ii)** By [[lem-hh-dihedral-root-recurrence-and-root-sign]] (4), $W$ is the infinite dihedral group with translation $u=st$ of infinite order and $sus^{-1}=u^{-1}$; $tst=u^{-2}s$ is a reflection since it is conjugate to $s$ ([[def-cg-canonical-reflection-homomorphism]]). Now $s\\cdot tst=u^2$ and $tst\\cdot s=u^{-2}$, so $\\langle s,tst\\rangle\\supseteq\\langle u^2,s\\rangle$, and conversely $tst=u^{-2}s\\in\\langle u^2,s\\rangle$, so the two subgroups agree. The set $\\{u^{2k}\\}\\cup\\{u^{2k}s\\}$ is a subgroup because $su^{2m}s=u^{-2m}$, and it is the kernel of the homomorphism $W\\to\\mathbb Z/2$ with $s\\mapsto0$, $t\\mapsto1$ (the parity of the number of $t$'s, equivalently $u\\mapsto1$ and $s\\mapsto0$), hence of index $2$ ([[def-group-homomorphism]]); it is infinite because $u^2$ has infinite order. By the definition of parabolic subgroups ([[def-cg-parabolic-quotient-and-two-sided-minima]] (4)) together with $W_I\\cap S=I$ ([[thm-hh-parabolic-minimal-representatives-and-length-additivity]] (2)), the parabolic subgroups of $W$ are the conjugates of $\\{1\\},\\{1,s\\},\\{1,t\\}$ and $W$, i.e. $\\{1\\}$, the subgroups $\\{1,r\\}$ with $r\\in T$, and $W$; an infinite index-two subgroup is none of these. No use of the Axiom of Choice is made.",
